---
/**
 * The site's bar. What it does, and why:
 *
 * 1. THE NAV IS VISIBLE ON DESKTOP. Behind a hamburger at every width —
 *    including 1440px, where there is roughly 900px of empty bar — it
 *    costs a marketing site the one thing its header is for: showing a
 *    first-time visitor what's on the page. So the drawer survives
 *    untouched below `2xl` and the links come inline above it.
 *
 *    CAVEAT, and it's a real one: the labels are long ("מתי אנחנו
 *    יכולים לעזור" is 22 characters). With the eight original entries
 *    they ran ~1030px at 16px, which together with the logo, the CTA
 *    and the gutters needed ~1500px — hence `2xl` (1536px) rather than
 *    `xl`. Two of those eight went ("יצירת קשר" and "הרשמה" are the
 *    CTA's job, not the nav's) and the row has since taken back two
 *    things that are not links: "בית"/"Главная", and the language
 *    switcher at the head of it. Whether `xl` would now fit wants
 *    measuring in the browser rather than arithmetic here — and the
 *    thing to measure is the RUSSIAN row, per the next paragraph.
 *    Below whatever breakpoint it ends up being, the drawer is the nav.
 *
 *    AND RUSSIAN IS LONGER THAN HEBREW. The bar renders whichever
 *    language the URL is in, and the Russian labels are wider than the
 *    Hebrew ones — both because Cyrillic sets wider than Hebrew at the
 *    same size and because the words are longer. That's the whole
 *    reason the Russian pages carry short `navLabel`s in their
 *    frontmatter ("Сенсорная интеграция", not "Что такое сенсорная
 *    интеграция"): the shortening happens in the content, where a human
 *    can judge it, rather than by truncation here. `2xl` is the widest
 *    breakpoint Tailwind offers, so if the Russian row still overruns
 *    it, the fix is shorter labels or a smaller `text-base` — not a
 *    wider gate. Measure before deciding which.
 *
 * 2. The logo drops from h-18/h-22 to h-14/h-16. At 88px inside a 112px
 *    bar it fills 79% of the header's height, which reads as an
 *    oversized asset rather than as a brand mark, and leaves the CTA
 *    looking undersized next to it.
 *
 *    THAT RATIO IS BACK, and it's fine, because it was never the ratio
 *    that was wrong. The bar has since come down to 80/88px around the
 *    same h-14/h-16 mark (tokens.css), so the logo fills ~70% of it
 *    again — but as a normal mark in a tight bar rather than an
 *    88px asset in a loose one. What the earlier version was buying
 *    with its extra 16-24px was empty white above and below a 56px
 *    glyph, on a sticky element that sits over every page. It's also the PETALS ONLY at every
 *    width, not just below `sm`: the home page's h1 is now the wordmark
 *    at `text-brand`, so a second, small "Sensorika" in the bar above it
 *    is the same word twice on one screen — and the mark is what has to
 *    be recognisable in a sticky bar anyway. `variant="mark"` rather
 *    than `responsive`, so the desktop bar carries what the phone
 *    already carried.
 * 3. The CTA is a pill on `coral-700`, the logo's red square darkened
 *    until it clears AA — see Button for why a pastel petal base
 *    can't carry a white label, and tokens.css for why the one filled
 *    button in the bar is the site's single non-teal interactive fill.
 * 4. The bar is FLAT WHITE WITH A PERMANENT SHADOW, at every scroll
 *    position. Three earlier versions are worth recording, because each
 *    was wrong for its own reason:
 *
 *    - transparent until the first scroll, then `teal-100/85` with a
 *      blur. Translucency over a busy hero is where sticky headers go
 *      to become unreadable, and the blur cost a compositing layer on
 *      scroll for a difference nobody could see.
 *    - opaque `teal-100` always, shadow on scroll. That fixed the
 *      readability, but a tinted bar reads as part of the ground rather
 *      than as chrome sitting above it.
 *    - white, shadow on scroll. The header then had two looks, and the
 *      flat one — the one you meet first — was the weaker.
 *
 *    White is the surface every card on the page already uses, so the
 *    header reads as the topmost of them; the shadow is what says it
 *    floats over the ground rather than being painted onto it. Neither
 *    fact depends on scroll position, so neither is animated any more.
 *
 *    (There IS a scroll listener again, for point 5 — but it decides
 *    where the bar is, never what it looks like. The white and the
 *    shadow are still constants.)
 *
 * 5. IT GETS OUT OF THE WAY ON THE WAY DOWN. This is a sticky bar over
 *    pages that are mostly long-form parent reading, so it hides while
 *    you scroll down and comes back the moment you scroll up: the
 *    reader gets the whole viewport for the text, and the nav is one
 *    upward flick away rather than a scroll back to the top.
 *
 *    Three cases override the direction, and each of them is a real
 *    bug if you leave it out:
 *
 *    - NEAR THE TOP the bar is always shown, so the first flick on a
 *      freshly opened page can't leave it looking headerless.
 *    - WHILE THE DRAWER IS OPEN the bar is pinned. The drawer hangs
 *      from the bottom of the header and the trigger is its close
 *      button (it's showing an X by then), so hiding the bar would
 *      take the only way out of the drawer with it.
 *    - ON `focusin` the bar comes back, because its links and its CTA
 *      are still in the tab order while it's translated off screen. A
 *      keyboard user must never be focused on something invisible.
 *
 *    The direction is read through a small threshold rather than off
 *    the raw delta: touch momentum and iOS rubber-banding emit tiny
 *    alternating deltas, and without it the bar flickers open and shut
 *    for the whole of a decelerating flick.
 *
 * The drawer's state machine, focus handling and skip link are COPIED
 * UNCHANGED from Header.astro — this component keeps the exact same
 * contract (`data-nav-open` on <html>, `aria-controls="site-nav"`,
 * `nav:close`), so the shipped SideNav.astro works with it as-is and is
 * what should be rendered alongside it.
 *
 * THE NAV NOW LEAVES THE PAGE. Its six entries used to be same-page
 * anchors; each one is a route of its own now (site.ts), which is what
 * makes this bar the thing that holds the site together rather than a
 * table of contents for one document. Two consequences here:
 *
 *   - the current entry is marked, with `aria-current="page"` and with
 *     its underline drawn at rest instead of on hover. The underline
 *     was already the bar's hover affordance, so "you are here" is that
 *     same mark held down — nothing new to learn, and it can't be
 *     confused with the CTA the way a filled pill could.
 *   - the lockup links to `homeHref` rather than a literal `/`, so
 *     site.ts owns where home is and this component doesn't.
 */
import Button from '../ui/Button.astro';
import LangSwitcher from './LangSwitcher.astro';
import Logo from './Logo.astro';
import WhatsappIcon from '../../assets/icons/whatsapp.svg';
import { currentLocale, ui } from '../../data/i18n';
import { getNavItems, hasWhatsapp, homeHref, whatsappHref } from '../../data/site';

const locale = currentLocale(Astro);
const t = ui(locale);

/* The six entries with their labels in THIS language. The labels come
   from each page's own frontmatter (see getNavItems), so the bar and the
   page it links to can't disagree about what the page is called. */
const nav = await getNavItems(locale);

const ctaLabel = hasWhatsapp ? t.whatsapp : t.contact;
const external = hasWhatsapp ? { target: '_blank', rel: 'noopener noreferrer' } : {};

/* Same comparison Nav.astro makes for the drawer, for the same reason —
   Astro serves these as directories, so the pathname carries a trailing
   slash the href in site.ts doesn't have. */
const trim = (path: string) => path.replace(/\/+$/, '');
const here = trim(Astro.url.pathname);
---

<a
  href="#main"
  class="sr-only focus-visible:not-sr-only focus-visible:absolute focus-visible:top-3 focus-visible:start-3 focus-visible:z-[60] focus-visible:rounded-control focus-visible:bg-white focus-visible:px-4 focus-visible:py-2 focus-visible:text-sm focus-visible:font-medium focus-visible:text-ink-900 focus-visible:shadow-elevated"
>
  {t.skipToMain}
</a>

<header
  data-site-header
  class="sticky top-0 z-50 bg-white shadow-card"
>
  <div
    class="flex h-header items-center justify-between gap-4 px-gutter lg:h-header-lg lg:px-gutter-lg"
  >
    <Logo class="h-14 lg:h-16" href={homeHref(locale)} variant="mark" />

    <!-- Inline nav, 2xl and up. Plain row, and it stays a plain row in
         both languages: the items are laid out in source order and the
         page's `dir` decides which end that order starts from, so the
         Hebrew bar runs right-to-left and the Russian one left-to-right
         with no per-language markup.

         THE SWITCHER LEADS THE ROW, for the reason Nav.astro gives for
         leading the drawer with it: on a site half of whose parents read
         Russian, the first thing the nav answers is which language
         you're in. It sits beside <nav> rather than inside its <ul> so
         the list stays a list of pages — same arrangement as the drawer,
         same reason.

         It keeps `size="bar"`, the tinted pill, rather than borrowing
         the links' bare text: the current LANGUAGE and the current PAGE
         are two different "you are here"s, and the underline is already
         spoken for by the second one. -->
    <div class="hidden items-center gap-3 2xl:flex">
      <LangSwitcher />

      <nav aria-label={t.primaryNav}>
        <ul class="flex items-center gap-1">
          {
            nav.map((item) => {
              const current = trim(item.href) === here;
              return (
                <li>
                  <a
                    href={item.href}
                    aria-current={current ? 'page' : undefined}
                    class:list={[
                      'nav-link rounded-pill px-3 py-2 text-base font-medium whitespace-nowrap transition-colors duration-[var(--duration-fast)] hover:text-ink-900',
                      current ? 'text-ink-900' : 'text-ink-700',
                    ]}
                  >
                    {item.label}
                  </a>
                </li>
              );
            })
          }
        </ul>
      </nav>
    </div>

    <!-- CTA then trigger, in that order, so under dir=rtl the trigger
         stays outboard at the bar's far left edge — the drawer opens
         from that same edge, and the whole gesture reads as the panel
         unfolding from under the button.

         THE SWITCHER IS NO LONGER ONE OF THESE. It travels with the nav
         now, which means the inline row above at 2xl and the drawer
         below it — so the bar carries two controls at every width
         instead of three at some of them, and the `max-sm:hidden` that
         used to drop it on a phone is gone with it. -->
    <div class="flex items-center gap-2 sm:gap-3">
      <Button
        href={whatsappHref()}
        size="sm"
        variant="primary"
        class="max-sm:size-10 max-sm:gap-0 max-sm:px-0"
        {...external}
      >
        <WhatsappIcon class="h-4 w-4 max-sm:h-5 max-sm:w-5" aria-hidden="true" />
        <span class="sr-only sm:not-sr-only">{ctaLabel}</span>
      </Button>

      <!-- Drawer trigger. Hidden once the inline nav appears — leaving
           both would give the same links two entry points. -->
      <button
        type="button"
        data-nav-toggle
        aria-controls="site-nav"
        aria-expanded="false"
        class="inline-flex h-10 w-10 items-center justify-center rounded-pill text-ink-800 ring-1 ring-ink-900/10 transition-colors duration-[var(--duration-fast)] hover:bg-paper-100 2xl:hidden"
      >
        <span class="sr-only">{t.menu}</span>
        <span class="toggle-bars" aria-hidden="true">
          <span></span><span></span><span></span>
        </span>
      </button>
    </div>
  </div>
</header>

<style>
  /* Underline that grows from the inline start, instead of a background
     pill — a pill per link would put eight competing surfaces in a bar
     that already has a filled CTA in it. */
  .nav-link {
    position: relative;
  }
  .nav-link::after {
    content: '';
    position: absolute;
    inset-block-end: 0.35rem;
    inset-inline: 0.75rem;
    height: 2px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: var(--color-teal-700);
    transform: scaleX(0);
    transform-origin: var(--nav-underline-origin, left);
    transition: transform var(--duration-base) var(--ease-standard);
  }
  :global([dir='rtl']) .nav-link::after {
    --nav-underline-origin: right;
  }
  .nav-link:hover::after,
  .nav-link:focus-visible::after,
  /* The current page's underline is simply the hover state left on. */
  .nav-link[aria-current='page']::after {
    transform: scaleX(1);
  }

  /* The header used to drop its shadow while the drawer was open:
     both were `teal-100`, so a shadow across the join broke up what
     was meant to read as one continuous surface. That reasoning died
     with the tinted bar — a white header above a `paper-100` drawer is
     two surfaces, and the shadow between them is the truth rather than
     a seam. Hence no nav-open override here at all now. */

  /* Hidden means MOVED, not removed. `transform` leaves the bar in the
     layout — so `--spacing-header` still describes the gap that the
     drawer's top and `scroll-margin-top` are measured against — and it
     animates on the compositor instead of laying out the page again on
     every scroll frame. */
  [data-site-header] {
    transition: transform var(--duration-base) var(--ease-standard);
  }
  [data-site-header][data-hidden] {
    transform: translateY(-100%);
  }
  /* The bar still hides; it just stops sliding. Someone who asked for
     less motion asked about the animation, not about the behaviour. */
  @media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
    [data-site-header] {
      transition: none;
    }
  }

  .toggle-bars {
    display: grid;
    gap: 4px;
    width: 1.25rem;
  }
  .toggle-bars > span {
    display: block;
    height: 2px;
    border-radius: var(--radius-pill);
    background: currentColor;
    transition:
      transform var(--duration-base) var(--ease-standard),
      opacity var(--duration-fast) var(--ease-standard);
  }
  :global(html[data-nav-open]) .toggle-bars > span:nth-child(1) {
    transform: translateY(6px) rotate(45deg);
  }
  :global(html[data-nav-open]) .toggle-bars > span:nth-child(2) {
    opacity: 0;
  }
  :global(html[data-nav-open]) .toggle-bars > span:nth-child(3) {
    transform: translateY(-6px) rotate(-45deg);
  }
</style>

<script>
  /* The drawer's state machine keeps the contract SideNav.astro is
     written against — `data-nav-open` on <html>, `aria-expanded` on the
     trigger, a `nav:close` event to ask for a close — so the two stay
     swappable. The hide-on-scroll below it is this component's own, and
     writes only to the header's own element. */
  const html = document.documentElement;
  const header = document.querySelector<HTMLElement>('[data-site-header]');
  const toggle = document.querySelector<HTMLButtonElement>('[data-nav-toggle]');

  /* Where the bar is, as opposed to what it looks like. It's an
     attribute rather than an inline transform so the stylesheet above
     keeps ownership of the animation and this keeps only the decision. */
  function setHeaderHidden(hidden: boolean) {
    header?.toggleAttribute('data-hidden', hidden);
  }

  function setNavOpen(open: boolean) {
    html.toggleAttribute('data-nav-open', open);
    toggle?.setAttribute('aria-expanded', String(open));
    html.style.overflow = open ? 'hidden' : '';
    /* An open drawer pins the bar: the drawer hangs off the bottom of
       it and the trigger is now the X that closes it. */
    if (open) setHeaderHidden(false);
  }

  toggle?.addEventListener('click', () => {
    setNavOpen(!html.hasAttribute('data-nav-open'));
  });

  document.addEventListener('keydown', (event) => {
    if (event.key !== 'Escape' || !html.hasAttribute('data-nav-open')) return;
    setNavOpen(false);
    toggle?.focus();
  });

  document.addEventListener('nav:close', () => setNavOpen(false));

  /* Below this many pixels a scroll is momentum or rubber-band rather
     than someone deciding to go somewhere. */
  const DIRECTION_THRESHOLD = 8;

  let lastY = window.scrollY;
  let ticking = false;

  function onScroll() {
    const y = Math.max(window.scrollY, 0);
    const delta = y - lastY;

    /* `lastY` is deliberately NOT updated when the threshold isn't met:
       the deltas of a slow drag have to accumulate against a fixed
       reference, or a gentle scroll would never move the bar at all. */
    if (Math.abs(delta) < DIRECTION_THRESHOLD) return;
    lastY = y;

    /* setNavOpen already showed the bar and owns it until it closes. */
    if (html.hasAttribute('data-nav-open')) return;

    /* Within the bar's own height of the top, hiding buys no reading
       room worth the page appearing to lose its header on flick one. */
    if (y <= (header?.offsetHeight ?? 0)) {
      setHeaderHidden(false);
      return;
    }

    setHeaderHidden(delta > 0);
  }

  window.addEventListener(
    'scroll',
    () => {
      if (ticking) return;
      ticking = true;
      requestAnimationFrame(() => {
        ticking = false;
        onScroll();
      });
    },
    { passive: true },
  );

  /* The nav links and the CTA stay tabbable while the bar is translated
     off screen, so focus arriving at one of them has to bring it back. */
  header?.addEventListener('focusin', () => setHeaderHidden(false));
</script>
